## The project you build in

The project at the root of this repository is already wired up, and its
`README.md` describes it. It is a TypeScript project built with Vite, tested with
Vitest, linted with ESLint, and formatted with Prettier. That toolchain is fixed
and supplied.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### What you implement

Everything under `src/`. The directory does not exist yet.

`index.html` loads `/src/main.ts` as its entry point, so that module is where
your build starts. Beyond that the structure is yours: the frame loop and the
canvas fit, keyboard input, audio, asset loading, the diagnostics overlay, the
scrolling camera, the debug and automation surface, the figures this
specification fixes, and the game itself.

The game's state is yours to shape. What it must carry is what this
specification's rules require of it, and `specs/instrumentation.md` states the one
contract fixed over it.

### What stays as it is

- `index.html`, the page and the canvas the stage is fitted into.
- The configuration: `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`,
  `eslint.config.js`, `.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`, and `.gitignore`.

`package.json` takes one kind of change: a dependency you genuinely need, added
along with the updated `package-lock.json`. Its existing entries and scripts stay
as they are.

### Every figure comes from this specification

Every number, identifier, key binding, cue name, and piece of screen copy this
specification fixes is stated here under a name. Anything this specification does
not name is yours to decide.
{{else}}
### What you implement

`src/game.ts`, and any new modules you add beside it under `src/`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `Game<DeepcoreState, DeepcoreDebugApi>`:
`initialize` builds the state and the debug surface once and returns them
together as `[state, debug]`, `update` takes the current state as a read-only
view (`DeepReadonly<DeepcoreState>`) and returns the next state, advanced against
the frame's delta time in seconds, and `render` is handed that next state,
read-only again, and draws it. The engine holds the state by value and replaces
it with what each `update` returns, so a frame builds the next state from the
current one rather than writing into it, and nothing but `update` and the debug
surface's poses advances the game. All three functions throw `"not implemented"`
as they stand. Implement them.
{{else}}
`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `GameDefinition<DeepcoreDebugApi>`: the game
instance class, a level registry holding the single level the game runs in, and
`startLevel` naming it. The game opens that one level and never opens another;
every screen is a value of the state's screen field, so the world and its game
state live for the whole session. The instance's `initialize` registers the
actions, defines the cues, and returns the debug surface. The level's game mode
runs the screens and the rules, and it adds a single player in its `beginPlay`,
whose controller is where the actions are read. The mode's `gameStateClass` is
`DeepcoreState`. The actors, components, and controllers you write draw that
state and drive it. As seeded, the definition is a stub whose instance throws
`"not implemented"`. Implement it.
{{/if}}

`src/game.ts` also declares and exports `DeepcoreState`, the game's whole
authoritative state, and `DeepcoreDebugApi`, the debug and automation surface
`specs/instrumentation.md` fixes. What the state carries is what this
specification's rules require of it; how it is laid out is yours to decide, and
the debug surface is the only contract fixed over it.

`src/game.ts` also exports `BACKGROUND`, a CSS color string: the stage
background. `src/main.ts` hands it to the engine as the color the canvas is
cleared to each frame, so the letterbox bars around the stage match the game
itself. The seeded stub exports a placeholder; replace it with the color your
build uses.

