### The build interface

`npm ci` followed by `npm run build` produces the complete static site into
`dist/` at the repository root, with an `index.html` at the root of that
directory as the entry point. That directory runs correctly when served as-is
from a static file server at any base path, not only at a server root, so every
URL the built site requests resolves relative to the page rather than the origin
root. `specs/assets.md` states that rule in full, and it governs the produced
asset files and the bundled JavaScript and CSS alike. The scripts that do this
are already in `package.json`.

The build is self-contained. `npm run build` bundles the produced asset files
committed to this repository and never invokes the asset-generation tools, which
are on the `PATH` only while this build is being made.

## Goal of this build

Produce a complete, polished, playable game that runs entirely in a browser. Aim
for a game a person would enjoy playing.

The build ships one expedition, played at a mode and a world size the player
chooses before it starts, and produces every sprite, animation, particle effect,
and sound it plays.

### Hard requirements

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Write the runtime the game stands on: the frame loop and its delta time, the
  canvas fit, keyboard input, audio, asset loading, and the debug overlay.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, taking the frame loop, the input actions,
  audio, asset loading, and the debug overlay from it.
{{else}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, taking the frame loop, rendering, the
  camera, the input actions, audio, asset loading, and the debug overlay from it,
  and writing the game inside its framework of game modes, actors, components,
  and controllers.
{{/if}}
- Render real graphics on the canvas. A text-only or character-grid rendering
  does not satisfy this requirement.
- Produce every sprite, animation, particle system, and sound the game plays with
  the six tools on the `PATH`, as `specs/assets.md` states, and load the produced
  files at runtime. The game ships no downloaded art or audio and draws no sprite
  it was asked to produce.
- Run in the browser with no backend. Everything needed to play is self-contained
  in the built site, and no API key or credential is needed to build, run, or
  play.
- Replace the starter `README.md` with one explaining what the game is, how to
  install dependencies, how to run it in development, how to produce the static
  production build, and the controls.

## Code quality

The code is fit for a long-lived codebase shared with human developers. The
type-check, lint, and format checks pass, and unit tests cover the simulation.

## Coordinate system and presentation

The stage is `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`, 16:9) logical units. The origin
`(0, 0)` is the top-left; `x` increases to the right and `y` increases downward.

- `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the
  browser window is the runtime's: the uniform scale that preserves the aspect
  ratio, the letterboxed centering, and the device pixel ratio. The complete
  stage is therefore on screen at every window size, on load and at any pixel
  density.
- The letterbox bars around the stage carry the stage's background color.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the runtime
  alone.

The stage is divided into two regions:

| Region | Extent | Holds |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Status bar | `y` in `[0, HUD_H]` (`0` to `56`), full width | The gauges and readouts `specs/ui.md` fixes. |
| Mine viewport | `x` in `[0, STAGE_W]`, `y` in `[HUD_H, STAGE_H]`, `VIEW_W x VIEW_H` (`1280 x 664`) | The world, seen through the camera `specs/world.md` fixes. |

The status bar is fixed and always fully visible. Menus and building panels open
as overlays over the viewport rather than as a persistent side panel.

The world is larger than the viewport, so it has a coordinate space of its own,
in the same logical units. A world position is converted to a viewport position
by subtracting the camera, as `specs/world.md` states.

Every rate in this specification is per second and every duration is in seconds,
integrated against the delta time the runtime hands each update. An interval of
game time reaches the same state however it was divided into frames, beyond the
drift a change in step size explains.

## Visual design

The mine is dark and industrial: a dim sky over a scrapped surface camp, and
beneath it earth giving way to gray rock, then near-black stone, then a
red-glowing shell around the planet's molten core. Choose the palette, the
typography, and the layout, and decide how the miner, the rock, the ore, the
buildings, and the effects are drawn.

Whatever look you choose, a player reads all of the following at a glance.

| What a player reads | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Depth band | The four bands' rock is drawn distinctly enough that a player descending tells one band from the next without reading the depth meter. |
| Solid against open | An unmined tile is clearly distinct from a carved tunnel. |
| Ore against rock | An ore vein stands out from the plain rock of its band, and the ten ores are told apart from one another. |
| Gemstone against ore | A gemstone reads as a rarer, richer find than an ore vein and is never mistaken for one. |
| Material node | A material node reads as distinct from both ore and gemstones. |
| Unbreakable stone | An unbreakable boulder reads as a harder material than the rock around it, so a player sees that the drill will not break it. |
| Lava | Lava is plainly visible as molten and dangerous against safe ground. |
| Gas | A gas pocket is drawn as ordinary rock. Its only tell is the faint seep `specs/hazards.md` fixes, subtle enough that a hurried dig misses it and visible enough that a player watching the tile catches it. |
| The prospector | The miner reads as a suited character carrying a drill and wearing a jetpack, the same character in every animation state, and its current state is readable from the frame on screen. |
| Text | Text is legible against its background at the logical stage size. |

The palette, the type, and every other aspect of the look are yours.
